<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altfrid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 874</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Altfrid of Hildesheim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Altfrid was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> from 851 until his death in 874 and is venerated as a saint. A churchman from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobility and an adviser to the East Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> Abbey as a community of canonesses around 845 to 852. He also built the first cathedral of Hildesheim, consecrated in 872.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>No contemporary biography of Altfrid survives. The Hildesheim Chronicle says he died rich in days, from which a birth around 800 is assumed. He owned land in the Harzvorland and in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, perhaps inherited, so he probably came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobility and may have been connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Liudolfings</a>. He was apparently a monk at Corvey Abbey before he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Hildesheim in 851, succeeding Ebbo, who had been installed and removed several times as archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. To avoid any doubt about their validity, Altfrid took the unusual step of repeating all his predecessor's consecrations and ordinations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Even before he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Altfrid helped to found several communities of women. Between 845 and 847 he obtained the relics of Saints Cosmas and Damian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and he helped the Saxon Count Ricdag found the nunnery at Lamspringe by bringing it relics of Saint Hadrian. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a>, he built a chapel of Saint Quentin and the church of the canonesses, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen-cathedral">Essen Cathedral</a>; the first abbess was his kinswoman Gerswith. He also founded Liesborn Abbey. His new cathedral at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, a three-aisled cruciform church, was consecrated on 1 November 872 before four bishops and the abbot of Corvey.</p><p>As a close confidant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, Altfrid took an influential part from 860 in the struggles of the disintegrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish empire</a>. He was present that year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> when Louis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> agreed a peace, and in the following years travelled widely, to Pitres in 864, the Synod of Thousey in 865, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 867 and the Synod of Worms in 868. He had a decisive influence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Treaty of Meerssen</a>, which divided Lotharingia between West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> on 9 August 870.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Altfrid died on 15 August 874 and was buried, as he wished, in the church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, where a Gothic tomb stands over his grave in the crypt named after him. Miracles were reported there around the year 1000. His cult faded after the abbey was secularised in 1803 and revived during the Kulturkampf, and in 1965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> confirmed his feast as an official festival of the new Bishopric of Essen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Altfrid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Essen Abbey was founded around 845 to 852 by <b>Altfrid</b>, later bishop of Hildesheim, as a community of canonesses for women of the Saxon nobility.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-essen">Imperial Abbey of Essen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=864" aria-label="Cologne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4708 397 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Altfrid"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5158 667h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5158 667h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=864">What was here in 864 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
